                <h2>This Week's Observation: Budget Allocation Fails</h2>
<p>On Tuesday, while reviewing an account for a home goods independent website team, we found an interesting budget structure: $200 daily budget split as $100 for Meta Dynamic Ads, $50 for TikTok video, and $50 for Google search and shopping. Sounds balanced, right? But diving into the data revealed the problem: the $100 Meta spend was running 2019-era creatives with a 0.6% CTR and $1.80 CPC, while the $50 TikTok budget only tested 2 video creatives with fewer than 3,000 impressions total.</p>
<p>This isn't an isolated case. Among 5 independent website accounts reviewed this week, 3 had similar issues:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Test phase budgets are artificially squeezed</strong>, leading to insufficient data samples for optimization</li>
<li><strong>Channel budgets allocated by "experience"</strong> without adjusting based on actual weekly performance data</li>
<li><strong>Creative test budget below 10%</strong>, leaving no new creatives when top performers burn out</li>
</ul>
<p>Let's break down these three most common pitfalls observed this week.</p>
<h2>Pitfall 1: Cutting Test Budgets Too Early</h2>
<p>Many teams confuse the order of "testing" and "scaling." The correct logic: small budgets for rapid experimentation → find combinations with ROI above 1.5x → then increase investment. But in practice, we see two extremes:</p>
<p><strong>First</strong>, closing campaigns after just 3 days when CTR is low, before the algorithm has learned your audience. This wastes prior progress.</p>
<p><strong>Second</strong>, test budgets set too low—running a new creative with only $5/day for 2 weeks, accumulating just 15 conversions. Impossible to judge if it's a "potential winner" or "junk."</p>
<h3>Adjustment: Establish Minimum Test Budget Standards</h3>
<p>Calculate on a weekly basis—your test campaigns need:<br />
- Per creative/audience combination: at least $50/week OR 30 conversions (use the higher threshold)<br />
- Test period: minimum 7 days, unless daily ROAS drops below 0.3 (then stop early)<br />
- Elimination rate: keep top 30% performers weekly, release budget from rest</p>
<p>One learning case this week: a utility app team fixed their weekly new creative test budget at 15% of total spend, using 3 small-budget campaigns ($20/day each) for different creative directions. By week 4, they found a video creative with ROAS of 3.2 and immediately scaled it in their main campaign.</p>
<h2>Pitfall 2: "Lazy" Channel Budget Allocation</h2>
<p>Many teams set budgets and don't check or adjust for a week. But channel CPCs fluctuate weekly—if Google search CPCs jumped 15% this week but your budget assumes last week's cost, ROI will drop.</p>
<p>A typical issue observed this week: a team put 60% of budget on Google search, but search competition intensified with CPCs rising from $0.80 to $1.20, while Meta CPMs dropped 20%. Shifting 30% budget from search to Meta could theoretically deliver 40% more impressions.</p>
<h3>Adjustment: Redistribute Budget Every Monday</h3>
<p>No complex calculations needed—just compare last week's data:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Channel</th>
<th>Last Week ROAS</th>
<th>This Week CPC/CPM Change</th>
<th>Recommended Action</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Meta</td>
<td>2.1</td>
<td>CPC flat</td>
<td>Maintain or slight increase</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>TikTok</td>
<td>0.8</td>
<td>CPM down 15%</td>
<td>Exploratory increase</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Google</td>
<td>1.4</td>
<td>CPC up 20%</td>
<td>Reduce accordingly</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Core principle: <strong>whoever outperforms the average gets more budget</strong>.</p>
<h2>Pitfall 3: Top-up Budget Misaligned with Spending Rhythm</h2>
<p>This issue is often overlooked but appeared multiple times this week: teams topped up $5,000 at month start, but ad spend accelerated faster than expected, leading to balance alerts mid-month. They had to reduce spend for days waiting for recharge, disrupting learning phases and tanking ROI.</p>
<p>The opposite extreme: month-start top-up followed by much slower-than-expected spend, leaving $2,000 unused at month-end—capital tied up unnecessarily.</p>
<h3>Adjustment: Plan Top-ups Based on Spending Curves</h3>
<p>Split monthly budget into 4 weekly top-ups:</p>
<ul>
<li>Week 1: 20-25% of monthly budget (test phase, slower spend)</li>
<li>Weeks 2-3: 35-40% of monthly budget (scaling phase, accelerating spend)</li>
<li>Week 4: 15-20% of monthly budget (optimization phase, spend slows as poor creatives are cut)</li>
</ul>
<p>If your account shows accelerating spend this week (e.g., a winning creative is live), add funds promptly. Don't wait for low-balance alerts.</p>
<h2>Editor's Note</h2>
<p>The key takeaway this week: budget allocation isn't a "set at month-start, review at month-end" static task—it requires dynamic adjustment following real-time data. Instead of obsessing over "what percentage should go to Meta vs. TikTok," spend 15 minutes weekly comparing channel performance and moving money from underperformers to top performers.</p>
<p>Next week we'll continue observing common issues in account structure optimization. Share your specific budget allocation challenges for discussion.</p>
